  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In a reversible reaction the forward and reverse reactions both occur, shown by the ⇌ sign.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both reactions continue, but at the same rate, so the concentrations stay constant.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Equilibrium is dynamic: the reactions continue in both directions, so it has not stopped.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There are four moles of gas on the left and two on the right, so higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The forward reaction is exothermic (ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹), so heating opposes it and shifts the equilibrium to the left.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Products go on top with their stoichiometric powers, reactants underneath.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]) = 0.64 ÷ (0.10 × 0.10) = 64.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    K_c rises with temperature, so heating drives the forward reaction: it must be endothermic.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Removing a product pulls the equilibrium to the right; K_c itself is unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A catalyst speeds both directions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner but at the same position.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The position of equilibrium moves in the direction that counteracts the imposed change.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A larger volume means lower pressure, which favours the side with more gas molecules — here two NO₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There are two moles of gas on each side, so changing the pressure shifts neither way.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Heating favours the endothermic reverse reaction, so less product is present and K_c falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    High pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules and low temperature favours the exothermic direction.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A compromise temperature is used: it is high enough for a workable rate but low enough to keep a reasonable yield.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Finely divided iron with a potassium oxide promoter catalyses the Haber process.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V₂O₅ catalyses the oxidation of sulphur dioxide to sulphur trioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only a modest pressure is needed, because the equilibrium already lies well to the right at 450 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Adding a reactant drives the forward reaction until a new balance is reached.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only the rates become equal, not the concentrations.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There are three moles of gas on the left and two on the right, so the units work out to mol⁻¹ dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    K_c = [B] ÷ [A] = 0.40 ÷ 0.20 = 2.0.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The forward reaction reduces three moles of gas to two, so higher pressure favours SO₃.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dissolving and crystallising continue at equal rates, which is the solubility equilibrium.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A large K_c means the product terms on top must be large, so the reaction has gone almost to completion.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lower pressure is opposed by producing more gas molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Pure solids and liquids have constant concentration; e.g., in CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), K_c = [CO₂].
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A tiny K_c means the reactants are favoured and little product is present.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cooling favours the direction that gives out heat, which here is the one producing the pale N₂O₄.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In the sealed flask evaporation and condensation continue at equal rates, so the amount of vapour is constant.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Extra product drives the reverse reaction, reducing the amount of product formed.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    High temperature and low pressure both oppose ammonia formation, so this pair gives the least ammonia.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Adding a reactant increases the forward rate, so the position moves to the right.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Without a catalyst both rates fall, but the equilibrium position remains the same.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Le Chatelier's principle describes the direction in which the position of equilibrium shifts.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    [HI]² = 64 × 0.20 × 0.20 = 2.56, so [HI] = 1.6 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dimer has half as many molecules, so higher pressure favours it.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    K_c depends only on temperature; the other changes shift the position but not the constant.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A high temperature and a catalyst both increase the rates, so equilibrium is established sooner.
  ]

]

